WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.389 --> 00:00:07.480
CBC News The National: Streets flooded, neighborhoods
submerged.

00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:11.740
NBC News: You couldn’t see anything but
water, nothing but water.

00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:16.910
CBS Mornings: This report by the world’s
leading climate scientists is released just

00:00:16.910 --> 00:00:18.790
once every 8 years, and it’s pretty grim.

00:00:18.790 --> 00:00:23.590
ABC News: What’s new in this report are
multiple adaptation strategies that can be

00:00:23.590 --> 00:00:29.939
successful if the global temperature rise
is limited to 1.5 degrees celsius.

00:00:29.939 --> 00:00:34.620
SIERRA: Adaptation is one of those words that
comes up in a lot of different contexts.

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:39.820
And it makes sense right, since the beginning
of time, all living things have been changing,

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:42.930
pivoting and adjusting in order to survive.

00:00:42.930 --> 00:00:48.980
SIERRA: When it comes to climate change, conversations
about survival have tended to focus on mitigation

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:56.290
- how the world can reduce and eventually
end its use of fossil fuels.

00:00:56.290 --> 00:01:04.540
SIERRA: But adaptation is the other half of
the equation.

00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:10.090
It means preparing our homes, our communities,
our infrastructure, and industries, to endure

00:01:10.090 --> 00:01:11.650
the effects of climate change.

00:01:11.650 --> 00:01:17.829
SIERRA: This month, a blockbuster report published
by the IPCC confirmed the extent to which

00:01:17.829 --> 00:01:20.479
devastating climate effects are now unavoidable.

00:01:20.479 --> 00:01:27.350
SIERRA: These challenges include more intense
forest fires, heat waves, hurricanes, landslides,

00:01:27.350 --> 00:01:28.350
and famine.

00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:32.360
Each of these dangers presents an enormous
adaptation challenge.

00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:36.750
SIERRA: One of the problems at the top of
the list was sea level rise.

00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:41.490
And that’s the part of adaptation that we’re
going to be talking about today - how countries

00:01:41.490 --> 00:01:46.710
can adapt in order to save the coastal cities
and the millions of people who live in the

00:01:46.710 --> 00:01:47.710
ocean’s path.

00:01:47.710 --> 00:01:51.720
SIERRA: I’m Gabrielle Sierra and this is
Why It Matters.

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:58.159
Today, adapting to rising tides.

00:01:58.159 --> 00:02:06.719
SIERRA: So where are we on the sea level rise
timeline?

00:02:06.719 --> 00:02:09.429
JACOB: Well, it began today.

00:02:09.429 --> 00:02:11.180
It began yesterday.

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:15.770
It only gets worse tomorrow and the day after
tomorrow.

00:02:15.770 --> 00:02:22.470
SIERRA: This is Klaus Jacob, he is a geophysicist,
a disaster and climate expert, and a professor

00:02:22.470 --> 00:02:24.150
at Columbia University.

00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:30.280
JACOB: The problem is that sea level rise
is actually accelerating.

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:34.450
It gets ever faster and faster in its rise.

00:02:34.450 --> 00:02:43.400
And so if we don't foresee that we will not
be able to keep up and so the earlier we start,

00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:44.860
the better we're off in the future.

00:02:44.860 --> 00:02:47.480
WCVB Channel 5 Boston: This morning, forecasting
our future.

00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:52.360
There is a new report painting a dire picture
when it comes to rising sea levels.

00:02:52.360 --> 00:02:55.569
Researchers are calling this new data a global
wake up call.

00:02:55.569 --> 00:03:00.450
CBS New York: The flood frequencies are going
to increase and they are going to become problematic.

00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:03.680
CNN: Coastal cities could flood even on sunny
days.

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:09.450
SIERRA: 40 percent of the world’s population
lives within 100 kilometers of the coast.

00:03:09.450 --> 00:03:11.349
SIERRA: One of those people is, me.

00:03:11.349 --> 00:03:16.799
I grew up in Manhattan Beach, Brooklyn, it’s
a small community nestled between the ocean

00:03:16.799 --> 00:03:18.160
and a bay.

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:22.659
Sandy flooded my community and many other
parts of New York City.

00:03:22.659 --> 00:03:28.640
The water reached heights of up to 14 feet,
and it destroyed a large portion of my childhood

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:29.640
home.

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:34.110
When I go back I still see collapsed houses,
and it’s a decade later.

00:03:34.110 --> 00:03:40.830
SIERRA: Hurricane Sandy was just one storm,
but it caused an estimated $70 billion dollars

00:03:40.830 --> 00:03:42.530
in damages.

00:03:42.530 --> 00:03:48.420
The cost of extreme weather and rising sea
levels is predicted to reach around $1 trillion

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:52.560
dollars by 2100, that’s in the US alone.

00:03:52.560 --> 00:04:00.129
JACOB: At low sea level, you need a very strong
storm to reach a certain height.

00:04:00.129 --> 00:04:06.390
But as sea level rises, you need ever smaller
storms to reach the same point.

00:04:06.390 --> 00:04:09.550
But smaller storms are more frequent.

00:04:09.550 --> 00:04:13.390
That means the same place floods ever more
frequently.

00:04:13.390 --> 00:04:18.389
Sandy, the last major hurricane that hit New
York City in 2012.

00:04:18.389 --> 00:04:24.300
That was roughly a storm with a probability
of 1 in 700.

00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:33.259
But now the frequency will increase by a factor
anywhere from 50 to 100 more often, the same

00:04:33.259 --> 00:04:35.139
place gets flooded.

00:04:35.139 --> 00:04:38.300
That's by the end of the century around there.

00:04:38.300 --> 00:04:48.600
So it's absolutely amazing how much a seemingly
modest sea level rise can amplify the flood

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:55.530
frequency and therefore the risks that we
incur in time.

00:04:55.530 --> 00:05:00.020
Sea level rise is not like a bathtub rising
everywhere the same.

00:05:00.020 --> 00:05:07.210
It rises differently in different parts of
the world, it's not only the global sea level,

00:05:07.210 --> 00:05:12.690
it's also the local sea level component that
often has a subsidence.

00:05:12.690 --> 00:05:17.139
SIERRA: Subsidence basically means that the
land is also sinking.

00:05:17.139 --> 00:05:19.819
Especially in regions with rivers or waterways.

00:05:19.819 --> 00:05:26.810
JACOB: For instance, in Deltas, like in the
Mississippi River Delta, New Orleans or in

00:05:26.810 --> 00:05:33.710
the Amazon delta or in the Nile or any other
major river, Rhine delta in the Netherlands.

00:05:33.710 --> 00:05:39.700
Even if there were no global sea level rise
from global warming, locally, you still would

00:05:39.700 --> 00:05:44.490
have an apparent sea level rise, which is
really the land sinking.

00:05:44.490 --> 00:05:51.610
But now you have both phenomena together and
that is really causing severe problems.

00:05:51.610 --> 00:05:56.690
SIERRA: Modeling is tough because sea level
rise depends on how much we mitigate.

00:05:56.690 --> 00:06:02.710
Today, global sea levels are predicted to
rise at least 1-3 ft by 2100.

00:06:02.710 --> 00:06:09.759
That’s a global average, so in some places
it could be lower or dramatically higher.

00:06:09.759 --> 00:06:11.880
For example, let’s go back to the big apple.

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:16.220
SIERRA: According to the NYC Panel on Climate
Change, the city could experience sea level

00:06:16.220 --> 00:06:19.599
rise of 6 ft by 2100.

00:06:19.599 --> 00:06:24.590
That means that without drastic changes, large
parts of the country’s most populous city

00:06:24.590 --> 00:06:25.919
could become uninhabitable.

00:06:25.919 --> 00:06:29.340
SIERRA: And this will be happening all over
the world.

00:06:29.340 --> 00:06:35.311
By the end of the century, sea level rise
could endanger or displace ​​410 million

00:06:35.311 --> 00:06:37.330
people from their homes.

00:06:37.330 --> 00:06:46.240
WAGNER: Frankly, sea level rise is an interesting
case, largely because it happens relentlessly

00:06:46.240 --> 00:06:49.169
and at an increasing rate.

00:06:49.169 --> 00:06:51.760
SIERRA: This is Gernot Wagner.

00:06:51.760 --> 00:06:56.760
Climate economist and professor at Columbia
Business School, on leave from NYU.

00:06:56.760 --> 00:07:02.780
WAGNER: On the other hand, the pace itself
is relatively low.

00:07:02.780 --> 00:07:09.139
So no, we don't have to move New York City
next decade because of sea level rise.

00:07:09.139 --> 00:07:16.110
What happens is that sea level rise makes
flooding events much, much more intense.

00:07:16.110 --> 00:07:18.660
Storm surges, much, much more intense.

00:07:18.660 --> 00:07:26.639
So even something as steady and relentless
as sea level rise has the most costly impact

00:07:26.639 --> 00:07:28.490
in terms of the extremes.

00:07:28.490 --> 00:07:36.840
As soon as we stop putting CO2 into the atmosphere,
as soon as we are at net zero emissions globally,

00:07:36.840 --> 00:07:38.950
temperatures will stop increasing.

00:07:38.950 --> 00:07:45.810
Well, if we were to achieve that temperatures
will stop increasing, sea levels will rise

00:07:45.810 --> 00:07:53.280
for decades, for centuries after and that
alone tells us how difficult a problem climate

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:55.080
change actually is.

00:07:55.080 --> 00:08:01.580
It is the most long term, most global, most
uncertain public policy problem out there,

00:08:01.580 --> 00:08:04.629
at least in the combination of those factors.

00:08:04.629 --> 00:08:11.810
JACOB: So adaptation can occur in fundamentally
different ways.

00:08:11.810 --> 00:08:14.700
There are essentially three.

00:08:14.700 --> 00:08:19.220
The first we can call protection, keep the
water out.

00:08:19.220 --> 00:08:24.759
The next can be accommodation, let the water
in and live with it.

00:08:24.759 --> 00:08:31.349
The third one, namely, is managed retreat
or strategic relocation.

00:08:31.349 --> 00:08:35.779
SIERRA: There are a few buckets when it comes
to addressing sea level rise.

00:08:35.779 --> 00:08:41.240
We can think of bucket one as seawalls and
barriers, which tend to be what you hear about

00:08:41.240 --> 00:08:43.940
most when it comes to adaptation.

00:08:43.940 --> 00:08:49.019
Even in my parents’ neighborhood, sea walls
have been the focus of many a very spirited

00:08:49.019 --> 00:08:50.339
community board meeting.

00:08:50.339 --> 00:08:57.720
JACOB: You can build seawalls on the shoreline
and levees and dikes but there are larger

00:08:57.720 --> 00:09:04.420
systems and they have successfully, in the
past, been applied in the Netherlands for

00:09:04.420 --> 00:09:08.260
instance, where they built entire barrier
systems.

00:09:08.260 --> 00:09:14.529
And they are largely open and only when a
storm comes, will they be closed.

00:09:14.529 --> 00:09:19.560
SIERRA: The queen of all barriers is in the
Netherlands and it’s called Maeslantkering,

00:09:19.560 --> 00:09:26.120
it is almost as long as the height of the
Eiffel Tower, and it weighs 4 times as much.

00:09:26.120 --> 00:09:31.420
There are all sorts of these barriers of different
types and sizes around the world.

00:09:31.420 --> 00:09:38.940
JACOB: And that has served in the past, the
Netherlands and some other places very well.

00:09:38.940 --> 00:09:46.930
But whenever you have a river coming out,
then these barrier systems have a problem.

00:09:46.930 --> 00:09:47.990
Why?

00:09:47.990 --> 00:09:54.500
Because they have to be ever more frequently
closed because even smaller storms eventually

00:09:54.500 --> 00:09:58.860
lead to the same flood level that you want
to keep out.

00:09:58.860 --> 00:10:06.519
But now we have a river coming out that wants
to get through the barriers out into the ocean.

00:10:06.519 --> 00:10:13.010
If the barriers are permanently closed, you
get it flooded from behind the barriers.

00:10:13.010 --> 00:10:17.870
That means it's not a sustainable adaptation
solution.

00:10:17.870 --> 00:10:25.080
SIERRA: Going back to New York, the city is
spending $1.45 billion dollars on something

00:10:25.080 --> 00:10:30.339
called the East Coast Resiliency Project,
a multipart barrier system that is set to

00:10:30.339 --> 00:10:32.680
wrap by 2026.

00:10:32.680 --> 00:10:37.620
In many cases these projects could provide
immediate protection, but some believe their

00:10:37.620 --> 00:10:42.480
capacity will be limited in decades to come,
if sea levels continue rising.

00:10:42.480 --> 00:10:47.889
And in other places, seawalls might not be
of much use even in the short term.

00:10:47.889 --> 00:10:58.680
SIERRA: All of this is a storm surge and it
keeps pouring into parts of downtown Miami.

00:10:58.680 --> 00:11:06.630
KLAUS: The geology of Florida is such that
it's made of very permeable limestone.

00:11:06.630 --> 00:11:11.250
And water can easily flow through that rock.

00:11:11.250 --> 00:11:17.310
So if you build a seawall on top, the ocean
doesn't care, it just flows underneath that

00:11:17.310 --> 00:11:21.540
seawall through the limestone behind the seawall.

00:11:21.540 --> 00:11:28.850
And so Florida right now is buying tens of
hundreds of millions of pumping systems and

00:11:28.850 --> 00:11:33.550
building some seawalls, which is a waste of
time and money.

00:11:33.550 --> 00:11:39.430
They should think about how either to build
floatable cities, if they are serious and

00:11:39.430 --> 00:11:45.260
trying to stay there, which is not easy, because
there are hurricanes coming in, they can drive

00:11:45.260 --> 00:11:52.089
them up or Floridians should think how soon
they want to sell their property and move

00:11:52.089 --> 00:11:53.640
up to Appalachia.

00:11:53.640 --> 00:11:58.839
SIERRA: As mentioned, on top of barriers,
there are other options, like nature based

00:11:58.839 --> 00:12:03.529
solutions, where we’d build out natural
landscapes for protection, one place doing

00:12:03.529 --> 00:12:05.709
this already is the Netherlands.

00:12:05.709 --> 00:12:12.310
OVINK: So climate adaptation is not only about
building a bigger wall against the flood,

00:12:12.310 --> 00:12:15.780
it is really about greening our infrastructure.

00:12:15.780 --> 00:12:23.279
SIERRA: This is Henk Ovink, he's a water ambassador,
more specifically a special envoy for international

00:12:23.279 --> 00:12:25.839
water affairs for the Kingdom of the Netherlands.

00:12:25.839 --> 00:12:31.209
Before this, he worked for President Obama's
Hurricane Sandy Rebuilding Task Force.

00:12:31.209 --> 00:12:35.720
We’ve been bringing up the Netherlands a
lot so far and this is why…

00:12:35.720 --> 00:12:40.180
OVINK: The Netherlands is a country that is
pretty vulnerable when it comes to flooding.

00:12:40.180 --> 00:12:44.340
A third of the country is below sea level,
60% is flood prone.

00:12:44.340 --> 00:12:47.639
The way we protect ourselves was not built
overnight.

00:12:47.639 --> 00:12:50.470
It's not that all of a sudden we thought,
"Oh, help.

00:12:50.470 --> 00:12:53.100
We are not flood prone, what are we going
to do?"

00:12:53.100 --> 00:12:54.100
No.

00:12:54.100 --> 00:13:00.899
In the ages coming from the 12th century and
even before, we started to develop measures

00:13:00.899 --> 00:13:06.190
in our rivers, on our coastlines that helped
us protect to the challenges that occurred

00:13:06.190 --> 00:13:07.190
then.

00:13:07.190 --> 00:13:13.889
And every time that measure was the baseline
for a next measure and a next measure.

00:13:13.889 --> 00:13:18.459
That type of redundancy in our environments,
in our cities, for that matter, coastlines

00:13:18.459 --> 00:13:20.130
help.

00:13:20.130 --> 00:13:26.220
So for instance, in the Netherlands along
our coast, our sea is battering our coastline.

00:13:26.220 --> 00:13:29.690
Because of that, we always have to replenish
our beaches.

00:13:29.690 --> 00:13:34.649
But instead of that now, we build an island
in front of the coast, an artificial island

00:13:34.649 --> 00:13:35.649
with sand.

00:13:35.649 --> 00:13:41.709
Now, because of the current of the ocean,
the sand is distributed along the coast and

00:13:41.709 --> 00:13:44.170
therefore we don't have to replenish it ourselves.

00:13:44.170 --> 00:13:49.370
But that doesn't work, let's say in Miami
where the coastline definitely is not sandy.

00:13:49.370 --> 00:13:52.790
You can't come up with such an approach like
we did in the Netherlands, so you have to

00:13:52.790 --> 00:13:54.410
think about totally different.

00:13:54.410 --> 00:13:59.490
And then if you go to Bangladesh where the
mangroves are protecting the impact of the

00:13:59.490 --> 00:14:05.279
floods and the hurricane cyclones that they
have there, those mangroves are battered by

00:14:05.279 --> 00:14:07.820
industrialization and pollution.

00:14:07.820 --> 00:14:14.410
But restoring those mangroves in Bangladesh
or Indonesia helps those coastlines become

00:14:14.410 --> 00:14:15.440
more resilient.

00:14:15.440 --> 00:14:21.519
So there are many ways across the world that
resiliency and adaptation builds redundancy,

00:14:21.519 --> 00:14:27.230
capacity, for coastlines and communities but
they are different everywhere.

00:14:27.230 --> 00:14:33.250
SIERRA: And these concepts are also really
important for how we rebuild after a disaster.

00:14:33.250 --> 00:14:39.360
OVINK: If you build back, you build back according
to new standards not to old.

00:14:39.360 --> 00:14:45.269
There's infrastructure investments being it
a house, a factory or a road, there are building

00:14:45.269 --> 00:14:46.269
codes.

00:14:46.269 --> 00:14:49.389
So there are flood maps, there is data and
analytics.

00:14:49.389 --> 00:14:54.660
So I think from government, private sector,
and community, there's a lot you can do.

00:14:54.660 --> 00:14:59.209
If the flood maps are not up to date, well,
you just have the wrong flood maps.

00:14:59.209 --> 00:15:04.870
If the data on the building codes is not future
proof, then the codes have to be made future.

00:15:04.870 --> 00:15:08.949
So there's many of those generic things that
you have to do.

00:15:08.949 --> 00:15:12.950
SIERRA: Ok, so that was bucket one - keeping
the water out.

00:15:12.950 --> 00:15:17.460
But at a certain point, those efforts might
not be sustainable.

00:15:17.460 --> 00:15:23.320
And that brings us to bucket two of sea level
adaptation - letting the water in.

00:15:23.320 --> 00:15:29.690
OVINK: I remember in the Netherlands, in our
large infrastructure program called Room for

00:15:29.690 --> 00:15:35.440
the River where based on flood events in the
90s we said, "We have to give the rivers more

00:15:35.440 --> 00:15:36.440
room again."

00:15:36.440 --> 00:15:42.610
So, we started not with the solutions, we
started with the assessment that we needed

00:15:42.610 --> 00:15:47.310
to make sure that our rivering system was
ready for that future.

00:15:47.310 --> 00:15:51.700
Also in the Netherlands people don't like
the government telling them to move away,

00:15:51.700 --> 00:15:54.410
we're no different than any other place around
the world.

00:15:54.410 --> 00:15:59.950
So we went into the communities and worked
with the farmers and the citizens.

00:15:59.950 --> 00:16:04.199
And what happened, there was one place that
we needed for flood retention.

00:16:04.199 --> 00:16:06.640
The farmers that lived there had to move.

00:16:06.640 --> 00:16:12.380
But we also provided the farmers with funding
to do research and come up with their own

00:16:12.380 --> 00:16:13.380
plan.

00:16:13.380 --> 00:16:18.120
So we provided them with experts and expertise
and capacity, and they came up with an alternative

00:16:18.120 --> 00:16:19.120
plan.

00:16:19.120 --> 00:16:24.220
And what was interesting, that alternative
plan worked better than just moving them away.

00:16:24.220 --> 00:16:28.960
Because they said, "We want to stay, this
is our farm and that of our fathers and grandfathers.

00:16:28.960 --> 00:16:33.230
And it has to be for our daughters and sons
and grandchildren."

00:16:33.230 --> 00:16:39.209
So instead of moving them away, we took the
farms apart and we built terps, a terp is

00:16:39.209 --> 00:16:40.850
like higher ground.

00:16:40.850 --> 00:16:44.709
Built new farms on top of them that were also
sustainably green.

00:16:44.709 --> 00:16:50.350
And now the farmers live in an environment
that can flood, that they can be isolated

00:16:50.350 --> 00:16:54.390
for a couple of days and then the water flows
again.

00:16:54.390 --> 00:16:55.880
They take that risk.

00:16:55.880 --> 00:16:57.560
But this is where they live.

00:16:57.560 --> 00:16:59.550
SIERRA: The idea is simple.

00:16:59.550 --> 00:17:04.190
Rather than fighting the water, you build
urban or rural infrastructure so that the

00:17:04.190 --> 00:17:07.410
water can flood in a controlled or planned
way.

00:17:07.410 --> 00:17:10.780
OVINK: I look at Harvey in Houston.

00:17:10.780 --> 00:17:15.900
The combination of a hurricane and a massive
rain event flooded the city.

00:17:15.900 --> 00:17:21.540
The rivering system had not enough capacity
to deal with both the surge that came in with

00:17:21.540 --> 00:17:23.419
the hurricane and the rain event.

00:17:23.419 --> 00:17:29.830
And the way the rivering system and the bayou
system was actually interrupted by infrastructure

00:17:29.830 --> 00:17:35.340
and housing ensured that the disaster became
also a disaster for the people that lived

00:17:35.340 --> 00:17:36.730
in Houston.

00:17:36.730 --> 00:17:41.549
And with that, you can rethink, you can really
assess that situation, say, "Okay, what did

00:17:41.549 --> 00:17:42.840
we do wrong?

00:17:42.840 --> 00:17:44.520
And how can we undo that?"

00:17:44.520 --> 00:17:50.580
By, again, creating more room for water, restoring
that bayou system that actually helped create

00:17:50.580 --> 00:17:53.240
redundancy in the water system.

00:17:53.240 --> 00:17:57.299
Don't build along the river in a way where
you're more vulnerable, but create more room

00:17:57.299 --> 00:17:58.900
for the river.

00:17:58.900 --> 00:18:01.750
Making sure it can meander again, grow and
shrink.

00:18:01.750 --> 00:18:05.590
And with that also create a healthier environment.

00:18:05.590 --> 00:18:10.700
Those are things we can do all over the world
to be able to prepare for the risks, and at

00:18:10.700 --> 00:18:14.830
the same time, create added value.

00:18:14.830 --> 00:18:21.830
SIERRA: There are other fascinating ideas
on how to live with the water too …

00:18:21.830 --> 00:18:25.799
JACOB: You can build floating cities.

00:18:25.799 --> 00:18:29.160
And the Dutch have already started to do that.

00:18:29.160 --> 00:18:34.789
And there are of course floating cities that
have been around for many, many centuries,

00:18:34.789 --> 00:18:36.270
go to Asia.

00:18:36.270 --> 00:18:41.549
In many harbors, you have essentially boats
where people live on and trade and do their

00:18:41.549 --> 00:18:42.630
business from.

00:18:42.630 --> 00:18:48.490
SIERRA: There is a whole new industry known
as aqua-tecture, do you get it?

00:18:48.490 --> 00:18:50.059
Like architecture.

00:18:50.059 --> 00:18:56.799
Anyway the idea is this, when a flood comes,
amphibious homes can float up with the water,

00:18:56.799 --> 00:19:01.980
but they’re anchored in such a way that
they come back down when the water recedes.

00:19:01.980 --> 00:19:09.620
JACOB: So that is a solution that in someplace,
will work better.

00:19:09.620 --> 00:19:15.860
I don't think it works very well for New York
City and Florida, and many other coastal areas

00:19:15.860 --> 00:19:23.169
in the Atlantic and in the Gulf because we
have these incredible wind storms with huge

00:19:23.169 --> 00:19:26.179
storm tides that come in.

00:19:26.179 --> 00:19:32.799
And so to keep floating communities afloat
and not make them drift away and bump into

00:19:32.799 --> 00:19:39.220
each other, that would be quite an engineering
feat and I haven't seen anybody trying that

00:19:39.220 --> 00:19:40.799
in these areas.

00:19:40.799 --> 00:19:45.940
SIERRA: Prevention and accommodation methods
can work together in different ways to meet

00:19:45.940 --> 00:19:52.030
rising sea levels, but some feel that even
this doesn’t go far enough.

00:19:52.030 --> 00:19:57.150
That brings us to bucket 3, strategic relocation
or managed retreat.

00:19:57.150 --> 00:20:01.460
It’s a matter of intense debate within the
adaptation community.

00:20:01.460 --> 00:20:07.610
HILL: One of the ways that humans have always
adapted is that people will move.

00:20:07.610 --> 00:20:13.040
SIERRA: This is Alice Hill, she’s a senior
fellow for energy and the environment at CFR.

00:20:13.040 --> 00:20:18.210
She served at the National Security Council
under President Obama, where she advised on

00:20:18.210 --> 00:20:21.430
catastrophic risks like climate change.

00:20:21.430 --> 00:20:29.580
HILL: Because if the land disappears from
underneath their feet or a wildfire occurs

00:20:29.580 --> 00:20:31.860
and they lose their home.

00:20:31.860 --> 00:20:39.360
Or if heat extremes or flooding wipes out
their crops or their livelihoods, those that

00:20:39.360 --> 00:20:47.030
can, will in many instances seek to find a
better life in another location.

00:20:47.030 --> 00:20:50.950
And we are seeing this dramatically across
the globe.

00:20:50.950 --> 00:20:55.450
We're already seeing higher numbers of people
on the move.

00:20:55.450 --> 00:21:01.390
Now, they are many reasons that they're displaced
as some referred to climate change as a threat

00:21:01.390 --> 00:21:04.980
multiplier or a crisis multiplier.

00:21:04.980 --> 00:21:11.330
Climate change adds an additional pressure
on people that can be the culminating event

00:21:11.330 --> 00:21:16.510
or just an added pressure that forces them
to the decision to move on.

00:21:16.510 --> 00:21:25.540
So we are seeing an estimated 50 million people
a year moving globally as a result of climate-worsened

00:21:25.540 --> 00:21:26.980
events.

00:21:26.980 --> 00:21:32.679
And as we go forward, their anticipation is
that this type of migration will increase

00:21:32.679 --> 00:21:38.720
in fact, to the point where our national intelligence
agencies have warned this may be one of the

00:21:38.720 --> 00:21:41.150
largest consequences going forward.

00:21:41.150 --> 00:21:46.230
And of course, when people are on the move
that creates security risks.

00:21:46.230 --> 00:21:52.020
For the people on the move, it creates tensions
among communities as there's conflicts over

00:21:52.020 --> 00:21:55.140
already restricted or reduced resources.

00:21:55.140 --> 00:22:01.860
You can think of many people moving into a
community where there's not enough freshwater,

00:22:01.860 --> 00:22:03.420
not enough food.

00:22:03.420 --> 00:22:10.159
And then there's a whole new set of needs
expressed by the people who are moving into

00:22:10.159 --> 00:22:11.539
the community.

00:22:11.539 --> 00:22:18.809
And we see that we are at risk of very chaotic
migration if we don't think about planning

00:22:18.809 --> 00:22:20.330
ahead.

00:22:20.330 --> 00:22:26.409
One country that is facing an existential
threat from climate change that's the country

00:22:26.409 --> 00:22:29.100
of Bangladesh, which is about the size of
Iowa.

00:22:29.100 --> 00:22:30.300
The state of Iowa.

00:22:30.300 --> 00:22:32.309
It has 200 million people in it.

00:22:32.309 --> 00:22:35.779
It's crisscrossed by rivers and then it has
a lot of coastline.

00:22:35.779 --> 00:22:39.950
So it's facing huge flooding risks for its
population.

00:22:39.950 --> 00:22:47.210
JACOB: There are some tens of millions of
peoples that will be displaced in Bangladesh

00:22:47.210 --> 00:22:48.210
alone.

00:22:48.210 --> 00:22:49.710
Where do they go?

00:22:49.710 --> 00:22:57.110
Well, not sure India or Malaysia wants to
have them suddenly coming into their country.

00:22:57.110 --> 00:23:02.140
And we are complaining about a few immigrants
from Central and South America coming into

00:23:02.140 --> 00:23:10.840
the US, well wait until sea level rise makes
millions of people, tens of millions of people

00:23:10.840 --> 00:23:12.320
knocking at our doors.

00:23:12.320 --> 00:23:15.710
SIERRA: It also means abandoning your home?

00:23:15.710 --> 00:23:22.030
JACOB: Your home, your heritage, your culture,
many things.

00:23:22.030 --> 00:23:25.830
And that is the big, big puzzle.

00:23:25.830 --> 00:23:31.890
Physically and financially, if we show the
political will, it's doable.

00:23:31.890 --> 00:23:38.090
But the social cultural aspect is the hardest
nut to crack.

00:23:38.090 --> 00:23:42.559
SIERRA: It can be hard to imagine this scale
of change.

00:23:42.559 --> 00:23:47.020
Potentially abandoning cities that define
our culture.

00:23:47.020 --> 00:23:54.059
Imagine massive crowds fleeing places like
New Orleans, Mumbai, Hong Kong, Manila, Tokyo

00:23:54.059 --> 00:23:55.590
and Shanghai.

00:23:55.590 --> 00:23:57.610
This could be the future we face.

00:23:57.610 --> 00:24:01.539
SIERRA: Moving entire cities would be totally
unprecedented.

00:24:01.539 --> 00:24:07.370
That’s why proponents of strategic relocation
think that we have to move quickly to put

00:24:07.370 --> 00:24:12.370
plans, and funding, in place, to figure out
how to do it in the best way possible.

00:24:12.370 --> 00:24:16.870
SIERRA: And that brings up an important part
of the adaptation picture.

00:24:16.870 --> 00:24:20.740
Whatever the policy, it’s going to cost
a lot of money.

00:24:20.740 --> 00:24:31.120
HILL: One 2019 estimate found that real estate
worth 1.4 trillion is already located within

00:24:31.120 --> 00:24:35.090
just 700 feet of the US coast.

00:24:35.090 --> 00:24:43.640
And that sea level rise alone is projected
to affect somewhere between four and 13 million

00:24:43.640 --> 00:24:46.070
Americans.

00:24:46.070 --> 00:24:51.710
Retreat from these coastlines would cost a
great deal of money.

00:24:51.710 --> 00:25:00.140
If just 1/10th of these people or buildings
retreated, it would cost an estimated $140

00:25:00.140 --> 00:25:01.790
billion.

00:25:01.790 --> 00:25:07.980
So we know that ahead we are going to face
some very costly choices about what to do

00:25:07.980 --> 00:25:14.860
to help communities that live in these areas,
already under threat as they make choices

00:25:14.860 --> 00:25:18.409
about how to live with a changing coastline.

00:25:18.409 --> 00:25:24.560
SIERRA: So what types of costs is climate
change overall already causing here in the

00:25:24.560 --> 00:25:25.560
US?

00:25:25.560 --> 00:25:32.899
HILL: The United States last year suffered
20 separate billion-dollar climate and weather

00:25:32.899 --> 00:25:39.000
disasters with total losses reaching $145
billion.

00:25:39.000 --> 00:25:46.110
So we are already paying a big price in terms
of climate change.

00:25:46.110 --> 00:25:50.070
And last year it wasn't just the United States
that was hit.

00:25:50.070 --> 00:25:57.390
Across the globe, the world incurred $329
billion in total economic losses from climate

00:25:57.390 --> 00:26:02.390
and weather catastrophes, according to some
insurers.

00:26:02.390 --> 00:26:08.340
And of course, without insurance and many
of these threats are not insured, the recovery

00:26:08.340 --> 00:26:15.770
costs fall on those who are harmed or on their
governments or philanthropies that are able

00:26:15.770 --> 00:26:17.920
and willing to help them.

00:26:17.920 --> 00:26:21.740
Swiss Re, which is one of the world's largest
reinsurers.

00:26:21.740 --> 00:26:29.320
Swiss Re projected last year that if we continue
on our current course of heating, world gross

00:26:29.320 --> 00:26:35.390
domestic product would drop by 18% by 2050.

00:26:35.390 --> 00:26:45.080
So we are not yet internalizing the costs
that come from climate impacts and their cascading

00:26:45.080 --> 00:26:51.800
casualties as a result of a flood that then
wipes out a community.

00:26:51.800 --> 00:26:53.650
Kids aren't going to school.

00:26:53.650 --> 00:26:55.510
There's more illness in the community.

00:26:55.510 --> 00:26:56.870
The tax base shrinks.

00:26:56.870 --> 00:27:02.929
All those things that happen, we could be
at risk for greater economic loss going forward

00:27:02.929 --> 00:27:06.159
and really on a very wide scale across the
globe.

00:27:06.159 --> 00:27:12.360
WAGNER: We are frankly very much still in
the mode of having the federal government

00:27:12.360 --> 00:27:19.000
come in and bail us out over and over and
over again which of course right from perspective

00:27:19.000 --> 00:27:21.700
of the homeowner, this is our social construct.

00:27:21.700 --> 00:27:23.309
This is our understanding.

00:27:23.309 --> 00:27:25.300
Catastrophe happens.

00:27:25.300 --> 00:27:30.840
President declares Federal Emergency, FEMA
comes in and helps.

00:27:30.840 --> 00:27:33.620
And that's a good thing.

00:27:33.620 --> 00:27:38.490
That's not necessarily stop this, but of course
it also comes with unintended consequences.

00:27:38.490 --> 00:27:44.450
Right, it comes with homeowners building themselves
in floodplains close to the coast where yes,

00:27:44.450 --> 00:27:50.540
sea level rise, storm surges, hurricanes will,
you know, at some point necessitate this sort

00:27:50.540 --> 00:27:55.669
of bailout and it's the government that comes
in and does so.

00:27:55.669 --> 00:27:58.200
That can't continue forever.

00:27:58.200 --> 00:28:07.169
Many more correlated events, climatic events
will certainly necessitate a rethinking of

00:28:07.169 --> 00:28:10.100
how do we bail out homeowners?

00:28:10.100 --> 00:28:18.409
How do we ensure that this sort of moral hazard
doesn't continue to happen forever.

00:28:18.409 --> 00:28:26.390
Right, so that starts with ideas like, we
can't always rebuild every home in its current

00:28:26.390 --> 00:28:27.390
place.

00:28:27.390 --> 00:28:33.840
Now that's an impossible political decision
just to be clear, very, very difficult for

00:28:33.840 --> 00:28:37.620
the mayor to say, "Sorry, you can't move back
there."

00:28:37.620 --> 00:28:40.500
There are always trade offs.

00:28:40.500 --> 00:28:45.080
Ever since philosopher Mick Jagger told us,
"You can't always get what you want."

00:28:45.080 --> 00:28:47.200
Right, of course, we know that.

00:28:47.200 --> 00:28:51.260
And so there are trade offs in the real sense.

00:28:51.260 --> 00:28:55.919
If you spend a trillion dollars on one thing,
it's not available for something else.

00:28:55.919 --> 00:29:02.910
Now, that's what makes this search for measures
that in fact, do more than one thing at a

00:29:02.910 --> 00:29:03.910
time.

00:29:03.910 --> 00:29:04.910
So important.

00:29:04.910 --> 00:29:06.010
JACOB: Yes.

00:29:06.010 --> 00:29:12.870
There will be choices and they will become
ever more difficult and urgent.

00:29:12.870 --> 00:29:15.179
Now here's one problem.

00:29:15.179 --> 00:29:20.510
Those with the resources for them, it's not
a difficulty to adapt.

00:29:20.510 --> 00:29:28.510
It's those folks without resources, within
our own cities, but also globally.

00:29:28.510 --> 00:29:34.350
And just think of these little atoll islands
in the Pacific and the Indian oceans, like

00:29:34.350 --> 00:29:35.960
the Maldive islands and so on.

00:29:35.960 --> 00:29:41.720
They were the ones that hardly contributed
to global warming.

00:29:41.720 --> 00:29:46.640
It's us, and yet they pay already the price.

00:29:46.640 --> 00:29:52.430
They are already having to leave their island
atolls.

00:29:52.430 --> 00:29:59.530
It is an equity issue because the people with
the least resources have the least power.

00:29:59.530 --> 00:30:08.360
And yet often there are for historical reasons
living in the most endangered, coastal areas

00:30:08.360 --> 00:30:12.970
because in their right mind, nobody would
build there in the first place.

00:30:12.970 --> 00:30:16.230
And then where would we put those people?

00:30:16.230 --> 00:30:21.190
We put them where nobody else wants to live
and the only places that are left over are

00:30:21.190 --> 00:30:24.309
those that are most endangered by flooding.

00:30:24.309 --> 00:30:30.450
SIERRA: And people with less resources, you
can't just afford to pick up and leave.

00:30:30.450 --> 00:30:32.180
JACOB: Of course not.

00:30:32.180 --> 00:30:40.700
And that's why we have to have a government
plan that assists those who need it most in

00:30:40.700 --> 00:30:47.890
the best possible way, which means we have
to first create public housing in high-lying

00:30:47.890 --> 00:30:49.490
areas.

00:30:49.490 --> 00:30:56.200
So when the day comes that they really have
to leave, the housing is already there that

00:30:56.200 --> 00:30:57.620
they can move into.

00:30:57.620 --> 00:31:02.409
WAGNER: Frankly, one of the most important
things to know about adaptation is the rich

00:31:02.409 --> 00:31:04.289
will adapt.

00:31:04.289 --> 00:31:08.720
Of course, so the rich jet off with their
private plane to higher ground.

00:31:08.720 --> 00:31:10.260
The poor will suffer.

00:31:10.260 --> 00:31:14.220
OVINK: Climate change is not an abstract future,
it is here and now.

00:31:14.220 --> 00:31:17.090
And the impacts are everywhere every day.

00:31:17.090 --> 00:31:23.370
Google a flood, a drought, a storm, a disaster,
and you find more than one every day somewhere

00:31:23.370 --> 00:31:24.779
around the world.

00:31:24.779 --> 00:31:25.779
Who suffers?

00:31:25.779 --> 00:31:28.960
The most vulnerable in every country around
a region.

00:31:28.960 --> 00:31:34.309
And in the context of a health crisis like
a pandemic, in the context of terrorism, in

00:31:34.309 --> 00:31:40.429
the context of economic decline and biodiversity
loss, those climate impacts are massive.

00:31:40.429 --> 00:31:47.520
They are just, sometimes the last event pushes
a community over the brink of collapse.

00:31:47.520 --> 00:31:52.909
Coming out of the climate conference, the
COP 26 in Glasgow, adaptation rose to the

00:31:52.909 --> 00:31:54.570
top of the agenda.

00:31:54.570 --> 00:31:55.580
Why?

00:31:55.580 --> 00:32:01.210
Because the most vulnerable, the most marginalized
of the world are impacted now not, tomorrow.

00:32:01.210 --> 00:32:06.840
We have to take action now to protect the
most vulnerable first and then build on that

00:32:06.840 --> 00:32:09.450
level of protection to protect the rest of
the world.

00:32:09.450 --> 00:32:15.190
SIERRA: Well, I mean, with that, I guess my
question is, is there a centralized authority

00:32:15.190 --> 00:32:19.220
in the US for coordinating and resourcing
such a massive effort?

00:32:19.220 --> 00:32:24.090
HILL: We do not have a central location for
all of these issues.

00:32:24.090 --> 00:32:28.150
I haven't heard much talk about creating a
new national department.

00:32:28.150 --> 00:32:34.250
I think a good place to start is simply creating
a national adaptation strategy, which is what

00:32:34.250 --> 00:32:40.350
we do in other areas where you have a multitude
of agencies with responsibilities to responding

00:32:40.350 --> 00:32:43.970
to the particular problem or crisis.

00:32:43.970 --> 00:32:46.790
On top of governance and institutional leadership.

00:32:46.790 --> 00:32:49.840
There needs to be buy-in at the community
level.

00:32:49.840 --> 00:32:54.780
And rallying this type of buy-in is a massive
challenge in and of itself.

00:32:54.780 --> 00:32:58.169
Trust me, I’ve seen it firsthand.

00:32:58.169 --> 00:33:04.340
SIERRA: How do you manage getting people on
board?

00:33:04.340 --> 00:33:09.380
Because I specifically think about my parents
in Manhattan Beach, which is an extreme flood

00:33:09.380 --> 00:33:10.380
zone.

00:33:10.380 --> 00:33:14.260
They were flooded by Sandy and the way that
my mother talks about their community group

00:33:14.260 --> 00:33:20.139
meetings, and you know, they can't even agree
on these measures within their own community.

00:33:20.139 --> 00:33:23.830
You know, how do you get people on board with
these solutions?

00:33:23.830 --> 00:33:28.620
OVINK: I remember when we started Rebuild
By Design and we started to reach out to the

00:33:28.620 --> 00:33:33.450
communities in the aftermath of Hurricane
Sandy, a lot of community said, "We don't

00:33:33.450 --> 00:33:36.720
really see the benefit of working with you,
and why?

00:33:36.720 --> 00:33:38.279
I mean, who are you?

00:33:38.279 --> 00:33:41.240
The government, we don't have that good experience."

00:33:41.240 --> 00:33:45.740
And that was even a nice way of what was said,
of course not always in the most friendly

00:33:45.740 --> 00:33:46.740
way.

00:33:46.740 --> 00:33:50.799
But we came back and said, "Okay, you know,
we need to work with you.

00:33:50.799 --> 00:33:56.049
It's not about us telling you what to do,
but it's about us together figuring out what

00:33:56.049 --> 00:33:58.210
is actually your needs.

00:33:58.210 --> 00:34:03.570
It's possible, but it's not easy, but that
it is not easy should not prevent us from

00:34:03.570 --> 00:34:04.610
trying.

00:34:04.610 --> 00:34:09.540
SIERRA: Nobody wants to have to go through
radical changes in their community.

00:34:09.540 --> 00:34:11.150
Nobody wants massive expenditures.

00:34:11.150 --> 00:34:16.590
And most of all... nobody wants to leave the
home or city they love behind.

00:34:16.590 --> 00:34:21.399
But some of these things will be necessary
because of the climate effects that are already

00:34:21.399 --> 00:34:23.060
baked into the equation.

00:34:23.060 --> 00:34:25.860
The time to start preparing is now.

00:34:25.860 --> 00:34:30.830
SIERRA: Still, the most extreme adaptation
measures don't have to happen.

00:34:30.830 --> 00:34:35.070
We can avoid them if we get way more serious
about mitigation.

00:34:35.070 --> 00:34:37.710
SIERRA: Remember, the two things are linked.

00:34:37.710 --> 00:34:41.200
The more we mitigate the less we will have
to adapt.

00:34:41.200 --> 00:34:47.150
And that’s why keeping warming at or below
1.5 degrees celsius is critical.

00:34:47.150 --> 00:34:51.119
OVINK: Without mitigation, there is no future.

00:34:51.119 --> 00:34:55.290
The world is on the brink of collapse with
four or five, six, seven, eight, nine degrees

00:34:55.290 --> 00:34:56.290
climate change.

00:34:56.290 --> 00:34:59.440
So without mitigation, it is like we die tomorrow.

00:34:59.440 --> 00:35:05.089
But without adaptation, we die today.

00:35:05.089 --> 00:35:17.550
